---
id: UR-001
type: rule
family: unsafe-rendering
title: Assignment to innerHTML / outerHTML
version: 1

severity: warning
confidence: medium
spectrum_position: 1

targets: [source]
file_extensions: [.ts, .tsx, .js, .jsx, .mjs, .cjs]
allowlist:
  [
    '**/*.test.ts',
    '**/*.test.tsx',
    '**/*.test.js',
    '**/*.test.jsx',
    '**/*.spec.ts',
    '**/*.spec.tsx',
    '**/*.spec.js',
    '**/*.spec.jsx',
    '**/__tests__/**',
    '**/tests/**',
    '**/explain/**',
  ]

detection:
  type: regex
  pattern: '\.(?:inner|outer)HTML\s*\+?=\s*(?:[^\s''"=]|[''"][^''"]*[''"]\s*\+)'

related: [UR-002, UR-003]
enabled: true
opt_in: false
---

You're assigning a string to `.innerHTML`/`.outerHTML`, which parses it
as live HTML. If any part of that string is attacker-influenced and
unsanitised, injected markup — `<img src=x onerror=...>`, inline
handlers, `javascript:` URLs — runs in the page's origin (DOM-XSS,
CWE-79).

If you're inserting text, assign `el.textContent = value` instead — it
never parses markup. If you need structure, build it with
`document.createElement` + `append` and set text/attributes individually.
If you genuinely need rich HTML from dynamic input, sanitise it with
DOMPurify immediately before the assignment and keep the two together.

This rule fires on assignment, not on `.innerHTML === ...` comparisons. It
skips a right-hand side that is a *pure* single- or double-quoted string
literal (`el.innerHTML = ''` to clear a node, or a static `'<b>…</b>'`
snippet you control), but it **does** fire on a literal that is then
concatenated with something else (`el.innerHTML = '<b>' + name`) — the
classic literal-prefixed DOM-XSS shape — as well as on template-literal and
identifier/expression right-hand sides. If a flagged value is in fact a
constant you control end to end, suppress UR-001 with a reason.
